BD-J: leave applications signalled PRESENT in the NOT_LOADED state

BDJLoader loads every application of the BDJO up front: AUTOSTART ones are
started, and everything signalled PRESENT gets proxys[i].init(). init() queues
CMD_INIT, which runs the Xlet's initXlet() and moves the proxy to PAUSED
(BDJAppProxy.java, "state = PAUSED").

A control code of PRESENT means the application is *available* in this title,
not that it should be loaded. Initialising it anyway is observable through
org.dvb.application.AppProxy.getState(), and a disc that inspects that state
before starting an application sees PAUSED where the specification leads it to
expect NOT_LOADED.

Measured on a retail BD-J disc (Rio, org 0x7fff646c). Its first-play title
autostarts a single application, com.macrovision.bdplus.Handshake, whose job is
to hand over to the real menu Xlet. Disassembling it (77000.jar) shows:

    atts = AppsDatabase.getAppsDatabase()
              .getAppAttributes(new CurrentServiceFilter());
    ... match by AppAttributes.getName() ...
    proxy = AppsDatabase.getAppProxy(id);
    if (proxy != null)
        if (proxy.getState() == NOT_LOADED) { "Starting other Xlet"; proxy.start(); }
        else                                  "ERROR: Application state wasn't
                                               NOT_LOADED. Can't load it."

Because libbluray had already initialised com.foxbd.dds.rio.fp.StandardMenuXlet
("Init xlet 4: ..."), its state is PAUSED and the handshake takes the error
branch. Macrovision's own logging is off on this disc, so nothing is printed:
the menu Xlet is simply never started. No AWT component is ever created (there
is no AWT-EventQueue thread in a thread dump, and all five BDJAppProxy threads
sit in Object.wait()), nothing is drawn, and the root window therefore refuses
to open the overlay:

    java.awt.BDRootWindow: sync() ignored (overlay not open, empty overlay)

which is why the player shows no menu at all while BD-J itself is running
perfectly.

Leave PRESENT applications alone. They are still in the apps database, still
enumerable, and still startable through AppProxy.start(), which loads and
initialises them on demand -- CMD_START goes through doStart(), not doInit().

--- a/src/libbluray/bdj/java/org/videolan/BDJLoader.java
+++ b/src/libbluray/bdj/java/org/videolan/BDJLoader.java
@@ -293,8 +293,11 @@
                     logger.info("Autostart xlet " + i + ": " + appTable[i].getInitialClass());
                     proxys[i].start();
                 } else if (code == AppEntry.PRESENT) {
-                    logger.info("Init xlet " + i + ": " + appTable[i].getInitialClass());
-                    proxys[i].init();
+                    /* Present, not loaded: leave the proxy in NOT_LOADED so
+                     * that getState() tells the truth to whoever asks. It is
+                     * started on demand through AppProxy.start(), which loads
+                     * and initialises it itself. */
+                    logger.info("Present xlet " + i + ": " + appTable[i].getInitialClass());
                 } else {
                     logger.info("Unsupported xlet code (" +code+") xlet " + i + ": " + appTable[i].getInitialClass());
                 }
